 ## Operating characteristics
-* **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$, where $N$ is the number of operations or loop length [S1, S2].
-* **Memory Complexity:** $O(\log N)$, where $N$ is the number of operations [S1, S2].
-* **Scaling:** Reduces memory complexity to $O(\log N)$, addressing scalability issues where exhaustive storage generates terabytes of intermediate state [S2].
-* **Strengths:** General applicability to any traced computation; significant memory savings for long sequences of operations.
-* **Limitations:** Increased computational overhead due to recomputation; not ideal for workloads with large checkpoint sizes (e.g., PBDR) [S2].
-* **When to Use:** When memory is the bottleneck and the computation is too large for exhaustive storage, and no custom adjoint is available.
-* **When Not to Use:** When custom adjoints are available (e.g., [Inversion Trick](964-inversion-trick.md), [Path Replay Backpropagation](6780-path-replay-backpropagation.md)).
+* **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$, where $N$ is the number of operations or loop length [S1, S2]. This is higher than the $O(N)$ time of exhaustive storage but acceptable for large $N$ [G].
+* **Memory Complexity:** $O(\log N)$, where $N$ is the number of operations [S1, S2]. This is a significant reduction from the $O(N)$ memory of exhaustive storage [G].
+* **Scaling:** Memory scales logarithmically with $N$, addressing the terabyte-scale intermediate state generation of modern processors that exhausts exhaustive storage [S2].
+* **Strengths:** General applicability to any traced computation; significant memory savings for long sequences of operations [G].
+* **Limitations:** Increased computational overhead due to recomputation; not ideal for workloads with large checkpoint footprints like PBDR [S2].
+* **When to Use:** When memory is the bottleneck and the computation is too large for exhaustive storage, and no custom adjoint is available [G].
+* **When Not to Use:** When custom adjoints are available (e.g., [Inversion Trick](964-inversion-trick.md), [Path Replay Backpropagation](6780-path-replay-backpropagation.md)), or when $N$ is small enough that exhaustive storage fits in memory [G].
 
